2026-09-30 21:02:28,686 src.graph.nodes INFO Agent input: {'messages': [HumanMessage(content='# Research Topic\n\nComprehensive Comparison of GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant in LLM Post-Training Quantization\n\n# Completed Research Steps\n\n## Completed Step 1: Accuracy Retention Comparison: Performance Degradation Across GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant\n\n<finding>\n# Comprehensive Comparison of GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant in LLM Post-Training Quantization\n\n## Problem Statement\nThis research aims to evaluate and compare the accuracy retention—specifically measured through perplexity, downstream task performance, and instruction-following capabilities—of three prominent post-training quantization techniques: GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant. The focus is on real-world large language models (LLMs) such as LLaMA, Mistral, and Qwen, under standardized quantization levels (4-bit and 8-bit) and evaluation tasks (natural language generation, reasoning, instruction following). The goal is to identify empirical results from peer-reviewed studies or industry reports that provide measurable performance degradation or retention across these methods.\n\n---\n\n## Research Findings\n\n### 1. Accuracy Retention and Perplexity Performance\nA peer-reviewed academic paper titled *"SmoothQuant: Accurate and Efficient Post-Training Quantization"* (arXiv:2211.10438) evaluates the performance of GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant on instruction-tuned LLMs across architectures ranging from 7B to 405B parameters. The study directly compares these methods using language modeling perplexity as a key metric. The results indicate that SmoothQuant demonstrates superior accuracy retention, particularly in 8-bit weight and 8-bit activation (W8A8) quantization, which enables high-fidelity performance with minimal degradation in downstream tasks.\n\nWhile the paper does not provide a direct side-by-side comparison of GPTQ and AWQ, it notes that both methods are effective for 4-bit quantization, with a focus on the accuracy-speed trade-off for enterprise deployment. The study emphasizes that GPTQ and AWQ are both well-suited for 4-bit compression, but their performance varies based on model architecture and use case.\n\n### 2. Methodological Differences and Design Principles\n- **GPTQ (Grouped Quantization)**: Focuses on group-level quantization of weights, aiming to preserve statistical distribution and reduce quantization error. It is effective for 4-bit models but may exhibit higher performance degradation in complex reasoning tasks.\n- **AWQ (Activation-aware Weight Quantization)**: Prioritizes weight quantization with activation-aware adjustments. It is designed to maintain activation values during quantization, which helps in preserving instruction-following performance. AWQ closely resembles SmoothQuant but differs in that it does not apply activation scaling.\n- **SmoothQuant**: A training-free, general-purpose post-training quantization method that scales weights before quantization. It enables 8-bit weight and 8-bit activation (W8A8) quantization while preserving model accuracy. SmoothQuant is shown to outperform both GPTQ and AWQ in accuracy retention, especially in models with complex reasoning capabilities.\n\n### 3. Evaluation on Real-World Models\nThe paper evaluates models such as LLaMA, Mistral, and Mixtral using simulated W8A8 quantization. The results demonstrate that SmoothQuant maintains the highest perplexity scores, indicating superior language modeling fidelity. In instruction-following benchmarks, models using SmoothQuant show consistent performance across diverse tasks, with minimal degradation compared to full-precision versions. GPTQ and AWQ show moderate performance, with GPTQ slightly outperforming AWQ in certain instruction-following scenarios due to better weight distribution.\n\n### 4. Industry and Practical Implementation\nThe TelperionAI Hugging Face repository provides practical implementations of Qwen models using both AWQ and GPTQ at 4-bit precision. This demonstrates real-world adoption and indicates that these methods are viable for deployment in local and enterprise environments. However, the repository does not include performance metrics or accuracy retention data, suggesting a gap in publicly available empirical validation for practical use cases.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\nBased on the available empirical evaluations, SmoothQuant demonstrates the best accuracy retention—particularly in perplexity and instruction-following performance—when applied to LLMs like LLaMA, Mistral, and Qwen at 8-bit (W8A8) and 4-bit levels. GPTQ and AWQ are effective for 4-bit compression, with GPTQ showing a slight edge in instruction-following tasks due to its group-level quantization strategy. However, both methods exhibit higher performance degradation compared to SmoothQuant, especially in complex reasoning and long-context tasks. The choice of quantization method should be guided by specific deployment goals: accuracy preservation, inference speed, and model size constraints.\n\nFor users prioritizing high-fidelity performance and minimal degradation, SmoothQuant is recommended. For those with limited computational resources or faster inference needs, GPTQ and AWQ remain viable options, though with a trade-off in accuracy.\n\n---\n\n## References\n- [Comparison: GPTQ vs AWQ vs SmoothQuant](https://apxml.com/courses/practical-llm-quantization/chapter-3-advanced-ptq-techniques/comparing-advanced-ptq)\n\n- [A Comprehensive Evaluation of Quantized Instruction-Tuned LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10438)\n\n- [LLM Quantization Review | Bitter Lesson](https://sherlock-dev.netlify.app/posts/2023-10-02-llm-quant/)\n\n- [TelperionAI/Qwen 3.8-27B-NVFP4-AWQ-GPTQ on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/TelperionAI/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4-AWQ-GPTQ)\n</finding>\n\n# Current Step\n\n## Title\n\nSpeedup and Inference Efficiency Analysis: Latency, Throughput, and Computational Overhead\n\n## Description\n\nGather data on the inference speedup (e.g., reduction in token generation latency, increase in throughput) achieved by GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant. Search for benchmarks comparing execution time, memory bandwidth usage, and model loading speed across different hardware platforms (e.g., NVIDIA GPUs, AMD GPUs, TPUs). Include information on any observed overhead due to quantization or activation handling.\n\n## Locale\n\nen-US', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), HumanMessage(content='IMPORTANT: DO NOT include inline citations in the text. Instead, track all sources and include a References section at the end using link reference format. Include an empty line between each citation for better readability. Use this format for each reference:\n- [Source Title](URL)\n\n- [Another Source](URL)', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, name='system')]}
